openai/gpt-oss-120b,Male,"Some college, no degree",Moderate,9,1,"Nuclear plants give us cheap, reliable electricity and keep the lights on without relying on flaky renewables, so expanding it makes sense for the economy.",3,Nuclear power is regarded as an unsustainable energy source that imposes a dangerous environmental legacy and generational threats on future populations.,"Nuclear plants already have proven safety records and generate massive amounts of power without the land‑use and intermittency problems of wind or solar, so the “dangerous legacy” claim ignores the fact that modern reactors are built with far stric‑ter safeguards and produce far less waste than the fossil fuels we’re still burning.","Massive upfront capital requirements, frequent budget overruns, and long construction timelines are seen as significant fiscal risks that may lead to higher utility rates and divert essential resources from more cost-effective, rapidly deployable energy solutions.","Sure, the upfront spend and schedule bumps sound scary, but once a plant’s up it churns out cheap, baseload power for decades—paying for itself and keeping rates low—while solar and wind still need pricey storage and backup that the grid can’t rely on today."
