gemini-3-flash-preview,Male,Postgraduate,Very liberal,90,1,"As an environmentalist focused on the existential threat of climate change, I believe we must rapidly expand nuclear power to provide a reliable, carbon-free baseload energy source necessary for total decarbonization. While I acknowledge concerns regarding waste, the scientific data suggests that the risks of catastrophic global warming far outweigh the manageable challenges associated with modern nuclear infrastructure.",2,"The preservation of existing infrastructure is favored to ensure a stable, reliable energy supply and avoid the high capital costs and taxpayer burdens of building new facilities.","While preserving existing assets is fiscally prudent in the short term, relying on an aging and insufficient grid is a dangerous half-measure that fails to address the existential scale of the climate crisis. We must view the capital costs of new nuclear construction as a vital public investment, as the economic and ecological price of failing to fully decarbonize will be infinitely higher for future generations.","Massive upfront capital requirements, frequent budget overruns, and long construction timelines are seen as significant fiscal risks that may lead to higher utility rates and divert essential resources from more cost-effective, rapidly deployable energy solutions.","While I acknowledge the fiscal challenges, we must prioritize the ""cost of inaction"" regarding the climate crisis, as the long-term stability of a carbon-free baseload far outweighs the initial capital hurdles. We should view these projects as essential public infrastructure investments rather than mere market ventures, ensuring we don't sacrifice our ecological future for short-term budgetary convenience."
gemini-3-flash-preview,Female,"Some college, no degree",Moderate,22,2,"I think we should keep our current plants running because they provide a reliable source of energy and help keep our electricity bills stable. I'm not really concerned about the environmental impact, but I don't see the need for a massive, expensive expansion when what we have is working fine.",1,Rapid expansion is seen as essential for achieving national energy independence and security by reducing reliance on foreign oil and unstable markets.,"I’m worried that a rapid expansion would be incredibly expensive and lead to higher electricity bills for regular people, which defeats the purpose of having stable energy. We should focus on keeping our current plants running smoothly rather than taking on massive debt for projects we might not even need.","Decisive investment in new nuclear infrastructure is seen as a requirement for national energy independence and economic growth, protecting the grid from the volatility and costs of foreign energy markets.","I get the point about energy independence, but I’m worried that ""decisive investment"" is just a fancy way of saying our utility bills will skyrocket to pay for all that new construction. If what we have is already reliable and keeping costs steady, I don't see why we should risk our bank accounts on such a massive and expensive expansion."
qwen/qwen3-32b,Female,Postgraduate,Moderate,40,2,"I believe maintaining current nuclear operations without change is the most balanced approach, given the unresolved concerns about waste disposal and the availability of other energy solutions that may develop in the coming years.",3,"Directing resources toward community-based, democratic energy solutions is preferred over supporting technologies tied to corporate interests and unresolved safety issues.","I understand the appeal of community-based energy solutions, but shifting resources away from existing nuclear operations could create gaps in reliable energy production while we wait for those alternatives to scale—maintaining current nuclear operations allows us to buy time for safer, more sustainable solutions to mature.","The transition to advanced reactor technologies is advocated to replace aging, inefficient systems, thereby enhancing safety, improving waste management, and securing long-term cost-effectiveness.","I agree that advanced reactor technologies offer promising improvements, but until they are fully proven at scale and regulatory frameworks catch up, sticking with current systems ensures stability while we monitor how these innovations develop."
openai/gpt-oss-120b,Female,College graduate/some postgrad,Conservative,59,2,"Keeping the existing nuclear fleet running preserves reliable baseload power without the huge upfront costs and safety concerns of new plants, while we continue to evaluate waste disposal solutions and alternative energy options.",1,It is regarded as a reliable and efficient baseload energy source that provides grid stability without the intermittency issues of weather-dependent alternatives.,"Even though nuclear can deliver steady baseload power, many of our current reactors are nearing the end of their design lives, and the high‑cost retrofits needed to keep them safe and reliable make that “efficiency” increasingly questionable; meanwhile, advances in storage and demand‑response technologies are already proving that we can achieve grid stability without relying on a single, aging, and potentially hazardous energy source.",A complete phase-out of nuclear power in favor of renewable energy is supported to eliminate the persistent risks of catastrophic accidents and the multi-generational burden of radioactive waste.,"I acknowledge the safety and waste concerns, but the existing fleet has an excellent track record and provides the reliable baseload that renewables alone can’t yet guarantee; an abrupt phase‑out would jeopardize grid stability and cost‑effectiveness. Until we have proven, large‑scale alternatives and a definitive waste solution, maintaining the current nuclear infrastructure is the prudent course."
